<article class="env-proposition" id="0CD2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CD2">Proposition <span data-tag="0CD2">53.13.7</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p > 0$. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a nonconstant morphism of proper smooth curves over $k$. Then we can factor $f$ as </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X \longrightarrow X^{(p^ n)} \longrightarrow Y  \]
</div>
  <p> where $X^{(p^ n)} \to Y$ is a nonconstant morphism of proper smooth curves inducing a separable field extension $k(X^{(p^ n)})/k(Y)$, we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X^{(p^ n)} = X \times _{\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k), F_{\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k)}^ n} \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k),  \]
</div>
  <p> and $X \to X^{(p^ n)}$ is the $n$-fold relative frobenius of $X$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/030K" data-tag="030K">9.14.6</a> there is a subextension $k(X)/E/k(Y)$ such that $k(X)/E$ is purely inseparable and $E/k(Y)$ is separable. By Theorem <a href="/tag/0BY1" data-tag="0BY1">53.2.6</a> this corresponds to a factorization $X \to Z \to Y$ of $f$ with $Z$ a nonsingular proper curve. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/0CCZ" data-tag="0CCZ">53.13.4</a> to the morphism $X \to Z$ to conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>